\section{Reproducibility}
\label{app:repro}

\paragraph{Data and code.}
The score matrices are the 13 files released with~\citep{zhang2025miss} under the MIT licence, at commit \texttt{55faff49}; only the data files and the model lists are used, so no benchmark text is used or redistributed; the benchmarks behind the matrices are cited in Section~\ref{sec:setup}.
The code, the instructions with environment, seeds, commands and run times, and the aggregate result tables behind every figure and table are in the \texttt{code/} directory delivered with this paper (\texttt{code/README.md}; the data are fetched by \texttt{code/scripts/00\_download.sh} at the pinned commit, not redistributed; \texttt{bash code/scripts/run\_all.sh} reruns all experiments); the scripts that draw every figure and table from the result tables, and the map from each script to its output, are in \texttt{figures/} (\texttt{sh figures/scripts/make\_all.sh}, about $40$\,s).
Per-draw predictions are too large for the repository and are regenerated by the same scripts.
The check against the published code (Appendix~\ref{app:public}) needs clones of the three public repositories (tinyBenchmarks, Anchor Points, the \texttt{py-irt} fork), is not among the default stages of \texttt{run\_all.sh}, and uses none of that code in ours.

\paragraph{Compute.}
Everything ran on CPUs, each job single-threaded on a shared host; the hours below are wall-clock hours of the jobs, an upper bound of their CPU time. The 38 main jobs (one per benchmark and split) took $3.3$ hours, of which interpolation took $1.4$, lineage $1.2$ and extrapolation $0.7$ (from $0.5$ to $21.6$ minutes per job, the longest for MMLU-Pro); the sensitivity splits took $1.4$ hours more, $4.7$ in all. The recalibrated-estimator runs of Appendix~\ref{app:recal} took $2.7$ hours ($64$ jobs each for $n=100$ and for the $2\%$ budget), the IRT-setting check of Appendix~\ref{app:public} $1.45$ hours ($48$ jobs, from $25$ seconds to about $10$ minutes each), and the runs of the published code $2.0$ hours (the longest about $24$ minutes).

\paragraph{Fitting and tuning settings.}
The two-dimensional IRT model is fitted on the sources with Adam (learning rate $0.05$, $300$ steps) under penalties $0.5\sum\theta^2+0.05\sum a^2+0.005\sum(c-c_0)^2$, with $c_0$ the logit of the smoothed source accuracy of the item and starts of $0.1\,\mathcal N(0,1)$; item parameters are not refitted on the target.
The grids are $\lambda\in\{0,0.25,0.5,0.75,1\}$ for the difference estimator, $k\in\{3,10,30\}$ for kNN, ridge $\alpha\in\{0.01,0.1,1\}$ and $\varepsilon\in\{0.1,0.3,0.6\}$ for the probability-proportional design; the choice uses two draws per design on the inner split (one for anchor and information-top selection, one run of CAT).
The main anchor runs use a light $k$-means (one initialisation, at most $25$ Lloyd iterations); every estimate is clipped to $[0,1]$, and the kNN model estimate is the mean of the full scores of the $k$ neighbours.
\paragraph{Seeds.}
All random draws derive from one base seed and the tuple (benchmark, split, fold, $n$, draw, design).

\paragraph{Independence from the work this task reserves.}
The task reserved the published study of the same question and asked that it not be retrieved or read.
We did not retrieve or read it.
While listing the supplied data files, we fetched the front page of the data repository's README at the pinned commit and printed its first $80$ lines; those lines summarise the conclusions of the reserved study and list the names of the methods its code implements.
Nothing else of that repository was read, and the summary was not used to choose the methods, the splits or the primary endpoint, which were written down afterwards and before any target was scored; the independence of those choices cannot be verified from the outside, and the reader should treat it so.
